\pard\tx2380\b0\fs28\fi-1960\li2380\fc0\cf0 DESCRIPTION	
\b DPSAddTimedEntry()
\b0  registers 
\i handler
\i0  as a ªtimed entry,º a function that's called repeatedly at a given time interval.  
\i period
\i0  determines the number of seconds between calls to the timed entry.  Whenever an application based on the Application Kit attempts to retrieve events from the event queue, it also checks (depending on 
\i priority
\i0 ) to determine whether any timed entries are due to be called.  
\i userData
\i0  is a pointer that you can use to pass some data to the timed entry.\

\fs20 \

\pard\tx2760\tx3140\tx3520\fs28\li2380\fc0\cf0 The function registered as 
\i handler
\i0  has the form:\

\fs20 \

\pard\tx3140\tx3900\tx4400\tx4900\tx5400\tx5920\tx6420\tx6920\tx7420\tx7920\tx8440\tx8940\tx9440\tx9940\tx10440\tx10960\f3\fs24\li2760\fc0\cf0 void
\i  *handler
\i0 (DPSTimedEntry 
\i tag
\i0 , double 
\i now
\i0 , void *
\i userData
\i0 )\

\fs20 \

\pard\tx2760\tx3140\tx3520\f0\fs28\li2380\fc0\cf0 where 
\i tag
\i0  is the timed entry identifier returned by 
\b DPSAddTimedEntry()
\b0 , 
\i now
\i0  is the number of seconds since some arbitrary point in the past, and 
\i userData
\i0  is the pointer 
\b DPSAddTimedEntry()
\b0  received when this timed entry was installed.\

\fs20 \

\fs28 An application's priority threshold can be set explicitly as an integer from 0 to 31 through a call to 
\b DPSGetEvent()
\b0  or 
\b DPSPeekEvent()
\b0 .  It's against this threshold that 
\i priority
\i0  is measured (note that 
\i priority
\i0  can be no greater than 30Ðthe additional threshold level, 31, is provided to disallow all inter-event function calls).  However, if you're using the Application Kit, you should access the event queue through Application class methods such as 
\b getNextEvent:
\b0 .  Although some of these methods let you set the priority threshold explicity, you typically invoke the methods that set it automatically.  Such methods use only three priority levels:\

\fs20 \

\fs4 \

\pard\tx6420\tx10440\f2\b\fs28\li2760\fc0\cf0 Constant	Meaning\

\fs6 \

\f0\b0\fs28 NX_BASETHRESHOLD	Normal execution\
NX_RUNMODALTHRESHOLD	An attention panel is being run\
NX_MODALRESPTHRESHOLD	A modal event loop is being run\

\fs20 \

\pard\tx2760\tx3140\tx3520\fs28\li2380\fc0\cf0 When applicable, you should use these constants as the value for 
\i priority
\i0 .  For example, if you want 
\i handler
\i0  to be called during normal execution, but not if an attention panel or a modal loop is running, then you would set 
\i priority
\i0  to NX_BASETHRESHOLD.  \

\fs20 \

\b\fs28 DPSRemoveTimedEntry()
\b0  removes a previously registered timed entry.\

\fs20 \

\fs10 \

\pard\tx2380\fs28\fi-1320\li2380\fc0\cf0 RETURN	
\b DPSAddTimedEntry()
\b0  returns a number identifying the timed entry or 
\f1 -
\f0 1 to indicate an€error.\

\fs20 \

