\pard\tx2380\b0\fs28\fi-1960\li2380\fc0\cf0 DESCRIPTION	
\b DPSCreateContext()
\b0  establishes a connection with the Window Server and creates a PostScript execution context in it.  The new context becomes the current context.  The first argument, 
\i hostName
\i0 , identifies the machine that's running the Window Server; the second argument, 
\i serverName
\i0 , identifies the Window Server that's running on that machine.  With these two arguments and the help of the Mach network server 
\b nmserver
\b0 , the Mach port for the Window Server can be identified.  If 
\i hostName
\i0  is NULL, the network server on the local machine is queried for the Window Server's port.  If 
\i serverName
\i0  is NULL, a default name for the Window Server is used.  \

\fs20 \

\pard\tx2760\tx3140\tx3520\fs28\li2380\fc0\cf0 The last two arguments, 
\i textProc
\i0  and 
\i errorProc
\i0 , refer to call-back functions (defined in the Client Library specification) that handle text returned from the Window Server and errors generated on either side of the connection. \

\fs20 \

\fs28 For an application that's based on the Application Kit, you could create an additional context by making this call:\

\fs20 \

\pard\tx3140\tx3900\tx4400\tx4900\tx5400\tx5920\tx6420\tx6920\tx7420\tx7920\tx8440\tx8940\tx9440\tx9940\tx10440\tx10960\f3\fs24\li2760\fc0\cf0 DPSContext  c;\
\
c = DPSCreateContext(NXGetDefaultValue([NXApp appName], "NXHost"),\
                   NXGetDefaultValue([NXApp appName], "NXPSName"),\
                   NULL,\
                   NULL);\

\fs20 \

\pard\tx2760\tx3140\tx3520\f0\fs28\li2380\fc0\cf0 This example queries the application's default values for the indentity of the host machine and the Window Server.  By doing this, the new context is created in the correct Window Server even if that Server is not on the same machine as the application process.  \

\fs20 \

\fs28 The context that 
\b DPSCreateContext()
\b0  creates allocates memory from the default allocation zone.  Also, when there's difficulty creating the context, 
\b DPSCreateContext()
\b0  waits up to 60 seconds before raising an exception.  If you want to change either of these parameters, use 
\b DPSCreateContextWithTimeoutFromZone()
\b0 .  Its two additional arguments let you specify the zone for the context to use when allocating context-specific data and a timeout value in milliseconds.\

\fs20 \

\b\fs28 DPSCreateNonsecureContext()
\b0  creates a ªnonsecureº context in which you can use PostScript operators that are normally disallowed.  The most significant of these are operators that let you write files.\

\fs20 \

\b\fs28 DPSCreateStreamContext()
\b0  is similar to 
\b DPSCreateContext()
\b0 , except that the new context is actually a connection from the client application to a stream.  This connection becomes the current context.  PostScript code that the application generates is sent to the stream rather than to the Window Server.  The first argument, 
\i stream
\i0 , is a pointer to an NXStream structure, as created by 
\b NXOpenFile()
\b0  or 
\b NXMapFile()
\b0 .  The
\i  debugging 
\i0 argument
\i  
\i0 is intended for debugging purposes but is not currently implemented.  
\i progEnc 
\i0 and
\i  nameEnc 
\i0 specify the type of program and user-name encodings to be used for output to the stream.  The last argument, 
\i errorProc
\i0 , identifies the procedure that's called when errors are generated.\

\fs20 \

\fs28 Few programmers will need to call either of these functions directly:  The Application Kit manages contexts for programs based on the Kit.  For example, when an application is launched, its Application object calls 
\b DPSCreateContext()
\b0  to create a context in the Window Server.  Similarly, to print a View the Kit calls 
\b DPSCreateStreamContext()
\b0  to temporarily redirect PostScript code from the View to a stream.\

\fs20 \

\fs10 \

\pard\tx2380\fs28\fi-1320\li2380\fc0\cf0 RETURN	Each of these functions returns the newly created DPSContext structure.\

\fs20 \

\fs10 \

\fs28\fi-1860 EXCEPTIONS	
\b DPSCreateContext()
\b0  and 
\b DPSCreateContextWithTimeoutFromZone()
\b0  raise a dps_err_outOfMemory exception if they encounter difficulty allocating ports or other resources for the new context.  They raise a dps_err_cantConnect exception if they can't return a context within the timeout period.\

\fs20 \

