\pard\tx2380\b0\fs28\fi-1960\li2380\fc0\cf0 DESCRIPTION	
\b DPSDefineUserObject()
\b0  associates 
\i index
\i0  with the PostScript object that's on the top of the€operand stack, thereby creating a user object (as defined by the PostScript language).  If€
\i index
\i0  is 0, the object is assigned the next available index number.  The function returns the new index, which can then be passed to a 
\b pswrap
\b0 -generated function that takes a user€object.\

\fs20 \

\pard\fs28\li2380\fc0\cf0 Warning:  To avoid coming into conflict with user objects defined by the Client Library or Application Kit, use 
\b DPSDefineUserObject()
\b0  rather than the PostScript operator 
\b defineuserobject
\b0  or the single-operator functions 
\b DPSdefineuserobject()
\b0  and 
\b PSdefineuserobject()
\b0 .\

\fs20 \

\pard\tx2760\tx3140\tx3520\b\fs28\li2380\fc0\cf0 DPSUndefineUserObject()
\b0  removes the association between 
\i index
\i0  and the PostScript object it refers to, thus destroying the user object.  By destroying a user object that's no longer needed, you can let the garbage collector reclaim the previously associated PostScript object.\

\fs20 \

\fs10 \

\pard\tx2380\fs28\fi-1320\li2380\fc0\cf0 RETURN	
\b DPSDefineUserObject()
\b0 , if successful in assigning an index, returns the index that the object was assigned.  If unsuccessful, it returns 0.\

