\pard\tx2380\f0\b0\fs28\fi-1560\li2380\fc0\cf0 SUMMARY	Send an encoded PostScript path to the Window Server\

\fs30 \

\fs28\fi-1960 DECLARED IN	dpsclient/dpsNeXT.h\

\fs30 \

\fs28\fi-1940\li2760 SYNOPSIS	void 
\b DPSDoUserPath(
\b0 void *
\i coords
\i0 , int€
\i numCoords
\i0 , DPSNumberFormat€
\i numType
\i0 , unsigned€char€*
\i ops
\i0 , int 
\i numOps
\i0 , void *
\i bbox
\i0 , int 
\i action
\b\i0 )\

\pard\b0\fi-380\li2760\fc0\cf0 void 
\b DPSDoUserPathWithMatrix(
\b0 void *
\i coords
\i0 , int€
\i numCoords
\i0 , DPSNumberFormat€
\i numType
\i0 , unsigned char *
\i ops
\i0 , int 
\i numOps
\i0 , void *
\i bbox
\i0 , int€
\i action
\i0 , float€
\i matrix
\i0 [6]
\b )\

\fs30 \

\pard\tx2380\b0\fs28\fi-1960\li2380\fc0\cf0 DESCRIPTION	
\b DPSDoUserPath()
\b0  and 
\b DPSDoUserPathWithMatrix()
\b0  send an encoded user path to the Window Server and then execute, upon that path, the operator specified by 
\i action
\i0 .  The use of these functions, rather than the analogous step-by-step path construction, is encouraged; rendering an encoded path is much more efficient than executing the individual PostScript operators that would otherwise be needed.\

\fs20 \

\pard\tx2760\tx3140\tx3520\fs28\li2380\fc0\cf0 An encoded user path consists of an array of coordinate values, a sequence of PostScript operators, and a bounding box specification.  The values in the coordinate array are used as operands to the operators; the operands are distributed to the operators in the order that they're given.  The resulting path must fit within the bounding box.  \

\fs20 \

\fs28 The coordinates, operators, and bounding box are given by the functions' first five arguments:\

\fs20 \

\fs28\fi-380\li2760 ·	The array of coordinate values is given by 
\i coords
\i0 . \

\fs20 \

\fs28 ·	
\i numCoords
\i0  is the number of elements in 
\i coords
\i0 .\

\fs20 \

\fs28 ·	 
\i numType
\i0  specifies the data type of the coordinates, as described below.  All the values in 
\i coords
\i0  must be of the same type.\

\fs20 \

\fs28 ·	
\i ops
\i0  is the sequence of PostScript operators, represented by constants as listed below. \

\fs20 \

\fs28 ·	The  bounding box is defined by the four coordinate values that you pass as an array in the 
\i bbox
\i0  argument.  These are passed as operands to the 
\b setbbox
\b0  operator.  (If you don't supply a 
\b setbbox
\b0  as part of the 
\i ops
\i0  sequence, one is inserted for you.) \

\fs20 \

\fs28\fi0\li2380 The following integer constants represent the data types that you can pass as the 
\i numType
\i0 €argument:\

\fs20 \

\fs4 \

\pard\tx6420\tx10440\f2\b\fs28\li2760\fc0\cf0 Constant	Meaning\

\fs6 \

\f0\b0\fs28 dps_float	single-precision floating-point number\
dps_long	32-bit integer\
dps_short	8-bit integer\

\fs20 \

\pard\tx2760\tx3140\tx3520\fs28\li2380\fc0\cf0 You can also specify 16- and 32-bit fixed-point real numbers.  For 16-bit fixed-point numbers, use 
\b dps_short
\b0  plus the number of bits in the fractional portion.  For 32-bit fixed-point numbers, use 
\b dps_long
\b0  plus the number of bits in the fractional portion. \

\fs20 \

\fs28 These constants are provided for 
\i ops
\i0 :\

\fs20 \

\pard\tx6420\tx10440\fs28\li2760\fc0\cf0 dps_setbbox\
dps_moveto\
dps_rmoveto\
dps_lineto\
dps_rlineto\
dps_curveto\
dps_rcurveto\
dps_arc\
dps_arcn\
dps_arct\
dps_closepath\
dps_ucache\

\fs20 \

\pard\tx2760\tx3140\tx3520\fs28\li2380\fc0\cf0 Once the user path has been constructed, the operator specified by 
\i action
\i0  is executed.  The value of 
\i action
\i0  is an index into Display PostScript's encoded system names; the following constants, provided as a convenience, represent the most commonly used actions:\

\fs20 \

\pard\tx6420\tx10440\fs28\li2760\fc0\cf0 dps_uappend\
dps_ufill\
dps_ueofill\
dps_ustroke\
dps_ustrokepath\
dps_inufill\
dps_inueofill\
dps_inustroke\
dps_def\
dps_put\

\fs20 \

\pard\tx2760\tx3140\tx3520\b\fs28\li2380\fc0\cf0 DPSDoUserPathWithMatrix()
\b0 's 
\i matrix
\i0  argument represents the transformation matrix operand used by the 
\b ustroke
\b0 , 
\b inustroke
\b0 , and 
\b ustrokepath
\b0  operators.  If 
\i matrix
\i0  is NULL, the argument is ignored.\

\fs20 \

\fs28 The following program fragment demonstrates the use of 
\b DPSDoUserPath()
\b0  as it creates and strokes a user path (an isosceles triangle) within a bounding rectangle whose lower left corner is located at (0, 0) and whose width and height are 200.\

\fs20 \

\pard\tx3140\tx3900\tx4400\tx4900\tx5400\tx5920\tx6420\tx6920\tx7420\tx7920\tx8440\tx8940\tx9440\tx9940\tx10440\tx10960\f3\fs24\li2760\fc0\cf0 short   coords[6] = \{0, 0, 200, 0, 100, 200\};\
char    ops[4] = \{dps_moveto, dps_lineto,dps_lineto, \
                    dps_closepath\};\
short   bbox[4] = \{0, 0, 200, 200\};\
\
DPSDoUserPath(coords, 6, dps_short, ops, 4, bbox, dps_ustroke);\

\fs20 \

\pard\f0\fs28\li2380\fc0\cf0 Note:  If an application calls 
\b DPSDoUserPath()
\b0  with large values (~10,000-20,000) of 
\i numCoords
\i0  and/or 
\i numOps
\i0 , it may generate a Display PostScript error.\

\fs20 \

