\fs10 \

\pard\tx2380\f0\b0\fs28\fi-1560\li2380\fc0\cf0 SUMMARY	Access events from the Window Server\

\fs30 \

\fs28\fi-1960 DECLARED IN	dpsclient/dpsNeXT.h\

\fs30 \

\fs28\fi-1940\li2760 SYNOPSIS	int 
\b DPSGetEvent(
\b0 DPSContext 
\i context
\i0 , NXEvent *
\i anEvent
\i0 , int 
\i mask
\i0 , double 
\i timeout
\i0 , int€
\i threshold
\b\i0 )
\b0  \

\pard\fi-380\li2760\fc0\cf0 int 
\b DPSPeekEvent(
\b0 DPSContext 
\i context
\i0 , NXEvent *
\i anEvent
\i0 , int 
\i mask
\i0 , double€
\i timeout
\i0 , int€
\i threshold
\b\i0 )
\b0  \
void 
\b DPSDiscardEvents(
\b0 DPSContext 
\i context
\i0 , int 
\i mask
\b\i0 )\

\fs30 \

\pard\tx2380\b0\fs28\fi-1960\li2380\fc0\cf0 DESCRIPTION	
\b DPSGetEvent()
\b0  and 
\b DPSPeekEvent()
\b0  are macros that access event records in an application's event queue.  These routines are provided primarily for programs that don't use the Application Kit.  An application based on the Kit should use the corresponding Application class methods (such as 
\b getNextEvent:
\b0  and 
\b peekNextEvent:into:
\b0 ) or the function 
\b NXGetOrPeekEvent()
\b0  so that it can be journaled.  
\b DPSDiscardEvents()
\b0  removes all event records of a specified type from the queue.\

\fs20 \

\pard\tx2760\tx3140\tx3520\b\fs28\li2380\fc0\cf0 DPSGetEvent()
\b0  and 
\b DPSPeekEvent()
\b0  differ only in how they treat the accessed event record.  
\b DPSGetEvent()
\b0  removes the record from the queue after making its data available to the application; 
\b DPSPeekEvent()
\b0  leaves the record in the queue.  \

\fs20 \

\b\fs28 DPSGetEvent()
\b0  and 
\b DPSPeekEvent()
\b0  take the same parameters.  The first, 
\i context
\i0 , represents a PostScript execution context within the Window Server.  Virtually all applications have only one execution context, which can be returned using 
\b DPSGetCurrentContext()
\b0 .  Applications having more than one execution context can use the constant DPS_ALLCONTEXTS to access events from all contexts belonging to them.\

\fs20 \

\fs28 The second argument, 
\i anEvent
\i0 , is a pointer to an event record.  If 
\b DPSGetEvent()
\b0  or 
\b DPSPeekEvent()
\b0  is successful in accessing an event record, the record's data is copied into the storage referred to by 
\i anEvent
\i0 .  \

\fs20 \

\i\fs28 mask
\i0  determines the types of events sought.  See the section ªTypes and Constantsº for a list of the constants that represent the event type masks.  To check for more than one type of event, you combine individual constants using the bitwise OR operator.\

\fs20 \

\fs28 If an event matching the event mask isn't available in the queue, 
\b DPSGetEvent()
\b0  or 
\b DPSPeekEvent()
\b0  waits until one arrives or until 
\i timeout
\i0  seconds have elapsed, whichever occurs first.  The value of 
\i timeout
\i0  can be in the range of 0.0 to NX_FOREVER.  If 
\i timeout
\i0  is 0.0, the routine returns an event only if one is waiting in the queue when the routine asks for it.  If 
\i timeout
\i0  is NX_ FOREVER, the routine waits until an appropriate event arrives before returning.  \

\fs20 \

\fs28 The last argument, 
\i threshold
\i0 , is an integer in the range 0 through 31 that determines which other services may be provided during a call to 
\b DPSGetEvent()
\b0  or 
\b DPSPeekEvent()
\b0 .  \

\fs20 \

\fs28 Requests for services are registered by the functions 
\b DPSAddTimedEntry()
\b0 , 
\b DPSAddPort()
\b0 , and 
\b DPSAddFD()
\b0 .  Each of these functions takes an argument specifying a priority level.  If this level is equal to or greater than 
\i threshold
\i0 , the service is provided before 
\b DPSGetEvent()
\b0  or 
\b DPSPeekEvent()
\b0  returns.  \

\fs20 \

\b\fs28 DPSDiscardEvents()
\b0 's two parameters, 
\i context
\i0  and 
\i mask
\i0 , are the same as those for 
\b DPSGetEvent()
\b0  and 
\b DPSPeekEvent()
\b0 .  
\b DPSDiscardEvents()
\b0  removes from the application's event queue those records whose event types match 
\i mask
\i0  and whose context matches 
\i context
\i0 .  \

\fs20 \

\fs10 \

\pard\tx2380\fs28\fi-1320\li2380\fc0\cf0 RETURN	
\b DPSGetEvent()
\b0  and 
\b DPSPeekEvent()
\b0  return 1 if they are successful in accessing an event record and 0 if they aren't.\

\fs20 \

\fs10 \

\fs28\fi-1540 SEE ALSO
\b 	DPSAddFD(), DPSAddPort(), DPSAddTimedEntry(), DPSPostEvent(), NXGetOrPeekEvent()\

\fs20 \

