\pard\tx2380\f0\b0\fs28\fi-1560\li2380\fc0\cf0 SUMMARY	Create an event\

\fs30 \

\fs28\fi-1960 DECLARED IN	dpsclient/dpsNeXT.h\

\fs30 \

\fs28\fi-1940\li2760 SYNOPSIS	int 
\b DPSPostEvent(
\b0 NXEvent *
\i anEvent
\i0 , int 
\i atStart
\b\i0 )\

\fs30 \

\b0\fs28\fi-1960\li2380 DESCRIPTION	
\b DPSPostEvent()
\b0  lets you add an event record to your application's event queue without involving the Window Server.  
\i anEvent
\i0  is a pointer to the event record to be added.  
\i atStart
\i0  specifies where the new record will be placed in relation to any other records in the queue.  If 
\i atStart
\i0  is TRUE, the event is posted in front of all others and so will be the next one your application receives.  If 
\i atStart
\i0  is FALSE, the event is posted behind all others and so won't be returned until events that precede it are processed.\

\fs20 \

\pard\tx2760\tx3140\tx3520\fs28\li2380\fc0\cf0 You can free, reuse, or otherwise mangle 
\i anEvent
\i0  after you've posted it without fear of corrupting the posted record.  
\b DPSEvent()
\b0  copies the record it receives and posts the copy.  \

\fs20 \

\fs28 Note that event records you post using 
\b DPSPostEvent()
\b0  aren't filtered by an event filter function set with 
\b DPSSetEventFunc()
\b0 .\

\fs20 \

\fs10 \

\pard\tx2380\fs28\fi-1320\li2380\fc0\cf0 RETURN	
\b DPSPostEvent()
\b0  returns 0 if successful in posting the event record; it returns 
\f1 -
\f0 1 if unsuccessful in posting the record because the event queue is full.\

\fs20 \

\fs10 \

\fs28\fi-1540 SEE ALSO
\b 	DPSSetEventFunc()\

