\pard\tx2380\f0\b0\fs28\fi-1560\li2380\fc0\cf0 SUMMARY	Allow dead key processing for a context's events\

\fs30 \

\fs28\fi-1960 DECLARED IN	dpsclient/dpsNeXT.h\

\fs30 \

\fs28\fi-1940\li2760 SYNOPSIS	void 
\b DPSSetDeadKeysEnabled(
\b0 DPSContext 
\i context
\i0 , int 
\i flag
\b\i0 )\

\fs30 \

\b0\fs28\fi-1960\li2380 DESCRIPTION	
\b DPSSetDeadKeysEnabled()
\b0  turns dead key processing on or off for 
\i context
\i0 .  If flag is 0, dead key processing is turned off; otherwise, it's turned on (the default).\

\fs20 \

\pard\tx2760\tx3140\tx3520\fs28\li2380\fc0\cf0 Dead key processing is a technique for extending the range of characters that can be entered from the keyboard.  In NEXTSTEP, it provides one way for users to enter accented characters.  For example, a user can type Alternate-e followed by the letter ªeº to produce the letter ªÝº.  The first keyboard input, Alternate-e, seems to have no effectÐit's the ªdead keyº.  However, it signals Client Library routines that it and the following character should be analyzed as a pair.  If, within NEXTSTEP, the pair of characters has been associated with a third character, a keyboard event record representing the third character is placed in the application's event queue, and the first two event records are discarded.  If there is no such association between the two characters, the two event records are added to the event queue.  \

\fs20 \

\fs28 See the 
\i NeXT User's Reference
\i0  manual for a listing of the keys that produce accent characters.\

\fs20 \

\fs60 \

\pard\f2\b\fs28\li2380\fc0\cf0 \

{{\NeXTHelpMarker25475 \markername NS_DEV_DOCFOR:objc_function:DPSSetEventFunc();}
