{{\NeXTHelpMarker26530 \markername NS_DEV_DOCFOR:objc_function:DPSSetTracking();}
¬}\f2\b\i0\ulnone\ql\fs28\fi0\li2380\gray0\fc0\cf0\up0\dn0 DPSSetTracking() \

\fs20 \

\fs10 \

\pard\tx2380\f0\b0\fs28\fi-1560\li2380\fc0\cf0 SUMMARY	Coalesce mouse events\

\fs30 \

\fs28\fi-1960 DECLARED IN	dpsclient/dpsNeXT.h\

\fs30 \

\fs28\fi-1940\li2760 SYNOPSIS	int 
\b DPSSetTracking(
\b0 int 
\i flag
\b\i0 )\

\fs30 \

\b0\fs28\fi-1960\li2380 DESCRIPTION	
\b DPSSetTracking()
\b0  turns mouse event-coalescing on or off for the current context.  If 
\i flag 
\i0 is 0, coalescing is turned off; otherwise, it's turned on (the default).\

\fs20 \

\pard\tx2760\tx3140\tx3520\fs28\li2380\fc0\cf0 Event coalescing is an optimization that's useful when tracking the mouse.  When the mouse is moved, numerous events flow into the event queue.  To reduce the number of events awaiting removal by the application, adjacent mouse-moved events are replaced by the most recent event of the contiguous group.  The same is done for left and right mouse-dragged events, with the addition that a mouse-up event replaces mouse-dragged events that come before it in the queue.\

\fs20 \

\fs10 \

\pard\tx2380\fs28\fi-1320\li2380\fc0\cf0 RETURN	
\b DPSSetTracking()
\b0  returns the previous state of the event-coalescing switch.\

\fs20 \

\fs60 \

\pard\f2\b\fs28\li2380\fc0\cf0 \

{{\NeXTHelpMarker27385 \markername NS_DEV_DOCFOR:objc_function:DPSStartWaitCursorTimer();}
¬}\f2\b\i0\ulnone\ql\fs28\fi0\li2380\gray0\fc0\cf0\up0\dn0 DPSStartWaitCursorTimer() \

\fs20 \

\fs10 \

\pard\tx2380\f0\b0\fs28\fi-1560\li2380\fc0\cf0 SUMMARY	Initiate a count down for the wait cursor \

\fs30 \

\fs28\fi-1960 DECLARED IN	dpsclient/dpsNeXT.h\

\fs30 \

\fs28\fi-1940\li2760 SYNOPSIS	void 
\b DPSStartWaitCursorTimer()\

\fs30 \

\b0\fs28\fi-1960\li2380 DESCRIPTION	
\b DPSStartWaitCursorTimer()
\b0  triggers the mechanism that displays a wait cursor when an application is busy and can't respond to user input.  In most cases, wait cursor support is automatic:  You'll only need to call this function if your application starts a time-consuming operation that's not initiated by a user-generated event.\

\fs20 \

\pard\tx2760\tx3140\tx3520\fs28\li2380\fc0\cf0 Client Library routines and the Window Server cooperate to display the wait cursor whenever more than a preset amount of time elapses between the time an application takes an event record from the event queue and the time the application is again ready to consume events.  However, when an application starts an operation that isn't initiated by an eventÐsuch as one caused by receiving a Mach message or by processing data from a file (see 
\b DPSAddPort()
\b0  and 
\b DPSAddFD()
\b0 )Ðthe wait cursor mechanism is bypassed.  To ensure proper wait cursor behavior in these cases, call 
\b DPSStartWaitCursorTimer()
\b0  before beginning the time-consuming operation.\

\fs20 \

\fs10 \

\pard\tx2380\fs28\fi-1540\li2380\fc0\cf0 SEE ALSO
\b 	DPSAddFD(), DPSAddPort(), setwaitcursorenabled\

\fs20 \

\fs60 \

